Read and set the Intel CPU package power envelope on FreeBSD.

FreeBSD can change CPU frequency through cpufreq(4) and powerd(8), but has no
interface to the processor's power budget. On a thermally constrained machine
that distinction matters: frequency capping limits the top of the range
whether or not there is headroom, while RAPL lets the processor manage its own
frequency inside a power envelope you choose.

It is also the only way on FreeBSD to answer how many watts a CPU is actually
drawing, which is often the fastest way to tell a cooling problem from a
configuration one.

Package domain only. DRAM, PP0 and PP1 domains and the PCI-mapped DPTF
interface are deliberately out of scope.

Note that many vendors lock the limit register in firmware, after which the
driver can measure but not intervene. The hw.intel_rapl.locked sysctl reports
this.
